  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\n  The central idea is to use a multi-resolution, gradient-based optimization method to minimize the maximum overlap of the step function h and its shifted versions. We leverage the observation that the optimal solution likely has a repeating structure with piecewise constants. This method combines global exploration with detailed local refinement to escape local optima. The bottleneck in previous approaches was the computational cost of evaluating the objective function due to the O(n\u00b2) complexity of the convolution for full lag searches. We address this by first identifying a reduced set of critical shifts through statistical analysis, focusing optimization on these dominant lags.\n\n  Mathematical Specification:\n  We minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx using n-point samples. The discrete objective is computed as (1/n) * max_k \u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i]*(1-h[i+k mod (2n)]) where we handle boundaries without circular convolution (since shifts can go beyond 2). The constraint \u2211_i h[i] = n/2 ensures the integral is 1 (with dx=2/n). We formulate the optimization as:\n    Minimize: max_{k\u2208critical_lags} (C_k)  where C_k is the overlap for lag k\n    Subject to: \u2211_i h[i] = n/2 and 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n  We use a convex combination of exponential penalties to approximate the maximum operation, transforming non-convex regions into manageable convex sections for each lag. The objective function becomes:\n    F(h) = (1/\u03b2) * ln(\u2211_{k\u2208S} exp(\u03b2*(C_k)))\n  where \u03b2 is a large parameter, S is a subset of critical lags (determined step-wise), andC_k is the discrete overlap for lag k.\n\n  Implementation Procedure:\n  1. Read input parameters and initialization (n from parent parent if provided, otherwise start with n=30 uniformly).\n  2. Compute initial critical shift set at low resolution (n\u224830). Update S by periodically re-scanning all lags with high statistical confidence.\n  3. Formulate the optimization problem as CVXPY program, varying S while the upper-level constraint handles alignment issues.\n  4. Solve with mixed-integer clique propagation algorithm or large-scale nonlinear programming solvers (e.g., IPOPT).\n  5. Update resolution adaptively: coarse to fine (n=30\u219231\u219232...) but with exponential parameterization.\n  6. Post-process solution with specialized rounding rules and continuation strategies.\n\n  Search Choices and Useful Variations:\n  - Initial Resolution: 50 (if no parent), then refine to 200 if improvement exists\n  - Critical Lags: Start with top-50 lags for computation budget\n  - Exponential Weights: \u03b2 from 100 to 100000 exponentially increasing\n  - Transition Steps: n from 50 to 200 in adaptive increments for robustness\n  - Penalization Strategy: Start with quadratic barrier, switch to log-barriers upon progress\n  - Clipping Operation: Project solution to feasible region after each sparse-matrix adaptation\n\n  Compute Budget and Robustness:\n  Time allocation: 50% for coarse-to-fine exploration (budget 550 seconds), then 50% for local refinement (550 seconds).\n  Resource Scaling: For large n, use n_max = (budget_remain * 0.4 * n_initial)\n  Fallback strategy: Preserve best solution found and use log-barrier default initialization\n\n  Validation and Return Contract:\n  Verify solution satisfies: \n    - ||h[i]|| \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n    - Sum(h) == n/2 (within tolerance)\n\n  Final plan contains:\n    - Evolving resolution strategy\n    - Critical lag selection based on historical performance\n    - Hierarchical optimization layers\n    - Sensible parameterization to ensure validity\n    - Robust fallback for failed CVXPY invocations",
